    {
      "id": 909773,
      "category": "researcher",
      "content": "The `ldn` system module runs a DHCP server while the console hosts a local-wireless **lp2p/LDN** session (for example, photo sharing or local session in Mario Kart Live: Home Circuit). On receiving a frame, it reassembles received data into a fixed stack buffer with `memmove(dst, src, total_size)` and **never checks `total_size` against the buffer size**. `src` is the global BPF capture buffer holding the frame exactly as it arrived; `total_size` is the sum of the parsed layer lengths, and the DHCP-layer length is attacker-controlled - so a single oversized DHCP frame from a Wi-Fi peer overflows the stack, overwriting the **saved return address and callee-saved registers**. On Switch, the function returns to an attacker-chosen address. Overwriting only the low 2 bytes of the saved LR redirects PC within a 64 KB window of the module's own code without knowing the ASLR base.",
      "updated_at": "2026-10-02T20:06:25.204Z",
